<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/1st-century-bce">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · War</p><h1 class="enc-title">Caesar's Civil War</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/49-bce">49</a>–<a href="/years/45-bce">45 BCE</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/1st-century-bce">1st century BCE</a> · Also known as Great Roman Civil War · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Caesar's Civil War was fought from 49 to 45 BCE between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/julius-caesar">Julius Caesar</a> and the conservative faction of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-senate">Roman Senate</a> led by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pompey">Pompey</a>. It began when Caesar crossed the Rubicon into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> in January 49 BCE; his victories at Pharsalus (48), Thapsus (46) and Munda (45) left him master of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> and opened the way to the end of the Republic.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/caesars-civil-war.jpg" alt="Caesar's Civil War" width="320" height="195" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Historicair, Ifly6 · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Map_of_the_Ancient_Rome_at_Caesar_time_(with_conquests)-en.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>The main issue was how <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/julius-caesar">Julius Caesar</a>, who had been in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaul">Gaul</a> for almost ten years, was to be reintegrated into Roman politics after accumulating immense power and wealth there. He had allied himself with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marcus-licinius-crassus">Crassus</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pompey">Pompey</a> in the First Triumvirate during his <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/consul">consulship</a> in 59 BCE, but the alliance frayed in the mid 50s BCE. By late 50 BCE both Caesar and the anti-Caesarians, among them Pompey and Cato, seemed to believe the other would back down, and trust had eroded.</p><p>On 7 January 49 BCE the Senate was persuaded to demand that Caesar give up his post or be judged an enemy of the state. Caesar crossed the Rubicon on the 10th or 11th of January, which marked the formal start of hostilities.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>Caesar marched west and in June 49 BCE defeated a Pompeian army under Lucius Afranius and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marcus-petreius">Marcus Petreius</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lleida">Ilerda</a>, which put all of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> under his control. His lieutenant Curio was defeated and killed at the Battle of the Bagradas River in August. In 48 BCE Caesar crossed the Adriatic, but <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pompey">Pompey</a> forced him to withdraw at the Battle of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/durres">Dyrrachium</a>. The larger <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-pharsalus">Battle of Pharsalus</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a> was won by Caesar, and Pompey's army disintegrated. Pompey fled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>, where he was murdered on arrival.</p><p>Caesar became involved in the dynastic dispute between Ptolemy XIII and Cleopatra, and then defeated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pharnaces-ii-of-pontus">Pharnaces II</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zela">Zela</a> in a few weeks. He crossed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a> on 25 December 47 BCE and defeated Metellus Scipio at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-thapsus">Battle of Thapsus</a> in 46 BCE. Cato and Metellus Scipio died by suicide soon after. In 45 BCE Caesar defeated the last Pompeians at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-munda">Battle of Munda</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>, led by his former lieutenant <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/titus-labienus">Titus Labienus</a>. The Spanish campaign was marked by atrocities, with Caesar treating his enemies as rebels.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>Caesar held four triumphs, over <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaul">Gaul</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>, Asia and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a>, and the Senate made him dictator perpetuo in 44 BCE. A group of senators, including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marcus-junius-brutus">Marcus Junius Brutus</a>, assassinated him on the Ides of March of that year. Some scholars, such as Erich Gruen, see the war, triggered by miscalculation rather than design, as the event that set off the collapse of the Republic. Others see it as a symptom of that collapse. Caesar's death left a power vacuum, and his heir Octavian eventually took complete control and formed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Roman Empire</a> as Augustus.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Caesar's civil war” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In <b>Caesar's Civil War</b>, the Pompeians under Publius Attius Varus and King Juba I of Numidia routed the army of Caesar's general Gaius Scribonius Curio near the Bagradas River, in present-day Tunisia.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/august-24">24 August in history</a></li></ul></section>
